    "created_at": "2025-05-23T16:56:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-05-23T16:56:16Z",
    "body": "> TAG's [Casing Rules](https://w3ctag.github.io/design-principles/#casing-rules) state that enumerated values for attributes should be lowercase, dash-delimited.\n\nPerhaps I'm reading it wrong, but it says this of \"Enumeration values\" and links to [\"no-referrer-when-downgrade\"](https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-referrer-policy/#dom-referrerpolicy-no-referrer-when-downgrade) as the example, which is defined explicitly as an `enum ReferrerPolicy {` and is a list of HTTP header field values. But `closedby` is an [\"enumerated attribute\"](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#attr-dialog-closedby), which rhymes but I'm not sure it's the same thing? There aren't too many examples of multi-word enumerated attribute values, but there are a few:\n\n- [`<th scope={rowgroup|colgroup}>`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-th-element:enumerated-attribute)\n- [`<link rel={all kind of multi-word things}>`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#linkTypes)\n\nMost of those don't use a dash. I'm in favor of leaving it as-is, given that this is shipped. It's roughly consistent (see list above) with others, and change is painful for developers. I'm not sure what the benefit (to developers) would be of renaming, having multiple values for a while, changing MDN, etc.",
